{"id": "871d2463-79f0-4918-89f5-5f5cc6970b22", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review only the auroradataapi cluster in us-east-1. I'm checking encryption at rest and in transit, network exposure, and IAM database authentication and Secrets Manager use. Everything will be read-only.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_RxA8pj1xIqoTGC0aiC7g7V", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "aurora-auroradataapi-sec-review", "agent_type": "generic", "aws_account_id": "012345678901_1", "preloaded_skills": ["verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Read-only security review of a SINGLE Aurora cluster, covering ONLY the three areas listed in criteria. Return concise findings with evidence (actual values observed) and a severity/status per check (PASS / FAIL / WARN / UNABLE TO DETERMINE), plus brief remediation for each non-pass.",
  "context": "User wants an RDS security review ONLY for Aurora cluster 'auroradataapi' (ARN [redacted ARN]), account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1. STRICT SCOPE: do NOT list, describe or scan any other clusters, instances, or regions. Only describe this cluster by identifier, its member instances (taken from the cluster's DBClusterMembers), and only the specific supporting resources they reference (its cluster parameter group, its VPC security groups, subnet group/subnets, KMS key, and the Secrets Manager secret(s) tied to this cluster). Do not call any mutating API. Do not use CloudTrail.",
  "resources": ["[redacted ARN]", "account 012345678901_1", "region us-east-1"],
  "timeframe": "Current state (as of 2026-11-19)",
  "criteria": "1) ENCRYPTION AT REST: StorageEncrypted, KmsKeyId (AWS-managed aws/rds key vs customer-managed CMK; if CMK check key rotation status and key state via kms describe-key / get-key-rotation-status for that key only), encryption of snapshots/backups if evident from cluster/instance data, Performance Insights KMS key if enabled. 2) ENCRYPTION IN TRANSIT: the cluster parameter group applied (DBClusterParameterGroup) and the value of require_secure_transport (Aurora MySQL) or rds.force_ssl (Aurora PostgreSQL) depending on engine; the engine/version; the CA certificate (CACertificateIdentifier) on each member instance and whether it is nearing expiry/outdated; note if parameter group is default (cannot be changed) and status of ParameterApplyStatus/pending-reboot. 3) NETWORK EXPOSURE: PubliclyAccessible on each member instance; the DB subnet group and whether its subnets have a route to an internet gateway (describe only those subnets' route tables); each VPC security group attached to the cluster: inspect ingress rules for 0.0.0.0/0 or ::/0, wide CIDRs, and the DB port; also note the port, DeletionProtection, and whether the HTTP endpoint (Data API, EnableHttpEndpoint) is enabled since the cluster name suggests Data API use. 4) IAM DATABASE AUTHENTICATION AND SECRETS MANAGER: IAMDatabaseAuthenticationEnabled; whether MasterUserSecret / ManageMasterUserPassword (RDS-managed secret in Secrets Manager) is in use and its KMS key; if Data API is enabled, look for Secrets Manager secrets associated with this cluster (only secrets whose name/description/tags clearly reference auroradataapi, or the MasterUserSecret ARN) and check rotation enabled/rotation schedule, last rotated date, and encryption key. Do NOT retrieve or print any secret values (never call get-secret-value). If you lack permission for any call, report 'UNABLE TO DETERMINE' for that check with the error, rather than guessing. Output a compact structured summary grouped by the three areas."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Read-only security review of Aurora cluster auroradataapi in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

